[
  {
    "lesson_id": "064",
    "trigger_word": "retreat",
    "trigger_card_number": 608,
    "title": "LESSON 64: HAVING SAID THAT",
    "header_color": "cyan",
    "pages": [
      {
        "page": 1,
        "total_pages": 2,
        "content": [
          "Lesson 54 taught you to READ participle",
          "clauses. Now you write them.",
          "",
          "Two actions, one subject, one sentence:",
          "\"They lost the harbor. They retreated.\"",
          "becomes",
          "\"Having lost the harbor, they",
          "retreated.\"",
          "",
          "-ing = at the same time, or just before.",
          "\"Turning the corner, she saw the queue.\""
        ]
      },
      {
        "page": 2,
        "total_pages": 2,
        "content": [
          "The rule that matters: the participle",
          "belongs to the MAIN subject.",
          "",
          "Right: \"Having lost the harbor, the",
          "army retreated.\"",
          "Wrong: \"Having lost the harbor, the",
          "retreat began.\" — a retreat cannot lose",
          "a harbor.",
          "",
          "Passive form: -ed, or being + -ed.",
          "\"Built in 1890, the mill still stands.\"",
          "",
          "Set phrases: having said that, given",
          "that, assuming that.",
          "",
          "Many languages join with a conjunction",
          "and repeat the subject; English drops",
          "both and leans on the participle."
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "lesson_id": "065",
    "trigger_word": "luggage",
    "trigger_card_number": 662,
    "title": "LESSON 65: THE MAN ARRESTED",
    "header_color": "cyan",
    "pages": [
      {
        "page": 1,
        "total_pages": 2,
        "content": [
          "A relative clause can lose its who or",
          "which AND its be, losing nothing else.",
          "",
          "\"The luggage WHICH WAS left on the",
          "platform\" becomes",
          "\"The luggage left on the platform\".",
          "",
          "\"The man WHO WAS arrested\" becomes",
          "\"The man arrested\"."
        ]
      },
      {
        "page": 2,
        "total_pages": 2,
        "content": [
          "This lets English stack description",
          "around a single noun:",
          "\"the luggage left on the platform at",
          "Crewe on Tuesday night\"",
          "",
          "The layers, in order:",
          "[determiner] [adjective] NOUN",
          "[prepositional phrase] [participle]",
          "",
          "Only who/which + be may drop. You",
          "cannot reduce \"the man who owns the",
          "shop\" — there is no be to lose.",
          "",
          "Many languages put the description",
          "BEFORE the noun; English hangs a long",
          "tail behind it."
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "lesson_id": "066",
    "trigger_word": "commitment",
    "trigger_card_number": 708,
    "title": "LESSON 66: THE INCREASE IN FEES",
    "header_color": "cyan",
    "pages": [
      {
        "page": 1,
        "total_pages": 2,
        "content": [
          "Academic English turns verbs and",
          "adjectives into nouns, then builds on",
          "them.",
          "",
          "commit -> commitment",
          "increase -> the increase",
          "decide -> the decision",
          "able -> ability",
          "",
          "\"The team committed\" becomes",
          "\"The commitment of the team\"."
        ]
      },
      {
        "page": 2,
        "total_pages": 2,
        "content": [
          "Why bother? The noun can take modifiers,",
          "so one sentence carries more at once:",
          "",
          "\"The commitment of the whole team",
          "explains the change in their results.\"",
          "",
          "Common endings: -ment, -tion, -sion,",
          "-ance, -ence, -ity, -ness, -al.",
          "",
          "Use with care. Too much nominalization",
          "thickens prose and hides who did what.",
          "",
          "Many languages nominalize freely too.",
          "What is English is the of-chain that",
          "follows: the increase in the cost of",
          "the repair of the roof."
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
]
